1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
It makes our cities glow, our factories hum, and our telecommunications networks buzz.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,000
With the urgency of a telegram and the force of an electric shock,

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:14,000
copper helped catapult the world into the modern era.

4
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:20,000
But this superconductor sparked some bitter battles between the two tycoons who wanted to control it.

5
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
Now, the Copper Kings on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:41,100
In the remote regions of the Rocky Mountain West,

7
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:44,680
two men controlled nearly all the country's copper production.

8
00:00:45,460 --> 00:00:49,500
One was a ruthless banker known for preying on the misfortune of miners.

9
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:54,800
The other, a self-made man with a legendary knack for knowing where to dig.

10
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:58,860
Both created huge empires and lived like kings,

11
00:00:58,861 --> 00:01:05,340
but they fought a ferocious and often personal battle that lasted nearly 25 years.

12
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:10,880
A battle that stretched from the cramped mine shafts thousands of feet below the earth's surface

13
00:01:10,881 --> 00:01:14,700
to the majestic dome that towers over the nation's capital.

14
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:26,480
In the early 1860s, copper was the last thing on the minds of miners in Montana or anywhere else.

15
00:01:28,860 --> 00:01:35,600
Gold fever was raging.

16
00:01:36,280 --> 00:01:38,740
Caught and spread by the California 49ers,

17
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:40,880
it had become a national epidemic.

18
00:01:41,940 --> 00:01:44,800
Fortune hunters by the thousands risked their lives

19
00:01:44,801 --> 00:01:47,640
crossing the great plains and perilous Rocky Mountains,

20
00:01:48,140 --> 00:01:50,080
chasing a dream and a fortune.

21
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:54,240
Of the 100,000 miners who poured into California,

22
00:01:54,780 --> 00:01:56,220
most came up empty-handed,

23
00:01:56,700 --> 00:01:58,260
but some struck it rich,

24
00:01:58,261 --> 00:02:01,240
and theirs were the stories that made it back east.

25
00:02:01,780 --> 00:02:10,080
The California rush of 1849 really made America go gold crazy.

26
00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:14,560
So much had happened out there, so many stories, so many people,

27
00:02:15,020 --> 00:02:18,780
fewer than people thought, but so many people got rich.

28
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:25,480
It was a tremendous boom to the economy, and it awakened this greed.

29
00:02:25,481 --> 00:02:32,300
If California made Americans gold crazy and greedy, it also made the nation rich.

30
00:02:33,180 --> 00:02:36,220
In the ten years after gold was discovered at Sutter's Mill,

31
00:02:36,740 --> 00:02:40,320
$500 million in precious metal flowed back east.

32
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:45,340
Gradually, California's river of gold dried up.

33
00:02:46,940 --> 00:02:49,860
But gold rush fever raged on.

34
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:54,840
Prospectors fanned out across the West, searching for the next big strike,

35
00:02:54,841 --> 00:03:01,620
the new mother load. Among the places they searched were the remote regions of

36
00:03:01,621 --> 00:03:06,600
western Montana. When loose flakes and nuggets called placer gold turned up in

37
00:03:06,601 --> 00:03:11,020
the creek beds of the mountains, boom towns like Bannock, Virginia City and

38
00:03:11,021 --> 00:03:17,000
Butte were soon crawling with prospectors. Well the initial rush to

39
00:03:17,001 --> 00:03:22,720
Montana began in 1862 and it really crowned in 1863-64. So it took place

40
00:03:22,721 --> 00:03:24,220
right in the midst of the Civil War.

41
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:28,380
It was one of the mid-sized to larger placer gold rushes.

42
00:03:29,780 --> 00:03:32,660
One of the first prospectors to arrive in Montana

43
00:03:32,661 --> 00:03:35,460
was an Easterner named William Andrews Clark.

44
00:03:36,260 --> 00:03:39,760
Clark arrived with brains and ambition, but not much else.

45
00:03:41,160 --> 00:03:45,760
Clark was born in a small rural farming community

46
00:03:46,340 --> 00:03:49,980
of Connellsville, Pennsylvania in 1839.

47
00:03:49,981 --> 00:03:52,880
he was the second of 11 children.

48
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:56,160
So you know that they didn't have a lot of money

49
00:03:56,161 --> 00:03:59,120
and he came to Montana with a pick and shovel

50
00:03:59,121 --> 00:04:01,060
and started panning for gold.

51
00:04:01,720 --> 00:04:04,140
Clark was 24 when he arrived in Montana.

52
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:06,860
He didn't waste time making friends

53
00:04:06,861 --> 00:04:09,000
or hanging out in the saloons and brothels

54
00:04:09,001 --> 00:04:11,000
that sprang up wherever miners were.

55
00:04:11,480 --> 00:04:12,960
He got right to work.

56
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:15,640
Well, Clark was certainly not a charismatic guy.

57
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:18,160
He was once described as being about as magnetic

58
00:04:18,161 --> 00:04:19,560
as last year's bird's nest.

59
00:04:19,980 --> 00:04:21,680
That's not entirely fair to Clark.

60
00:04:22,240 --> 00:04:24,480
If you ever look at the pictures of Clark, though,

61
00:04:24,620 --> 00:04:28,180
the piercing dark eyes, the remarkable intelligence,

62
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:31,780
and an ambition that was almost beyond description.

63
00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:35,300
Panning for gold was arduous work.

64
00:04:35,980 --> 00:04:38,360
Standing in the frigid, rocky mountain streams,

65
00:04:38,780 --> 00:04:41,860
sifting through tons of sand and gravel day after day,

66
00:04:42,300 --> 00:04:44,600
straining to see a few golden specks.

67
00:04:46,200 --> 00:04:48,920
Clark started panning in the summer of 1863.

68
00:04:49,640 --> 00:04:52,580
By fall, he'd panned $2,000 worth of gold.

69
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:54,780
And for Clark, that was enough.

70
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:57,820
Not enough gold, enough hard work.

71
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:04,860
Clark had noticed that it wasn't the miners who were prospering off the gold rush.

72
00:05:05,180 --> 00:05:06,320
It was the merchants.

73
00:05:07,100 --> 00:05:11,980
The ones charging $100 or more for a one-pound bag of salt.

74
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:14,220
As you can check in the history books,

75
00:05:14,340 --> 00:05:17,800
it was the merchant that ended up having the gold that the miners took out of the ground.

76
00:05:17,801 --> 00:05:20,800
and Clark is surely no exception, actually.

77
00:05:20,940 --> 00:05:23,620
He's a prime example of how that did work.

78
00:05:23,900 --> 00:05:26,580
He was always thinking about how he could make a dollar

79
00:05:26,581 --> 00:05:31,660
and so even as young as 24, that's how old he was when he came,

80
00:05:32,140 --> 00:05:35,080
he was carrying mail, doing mail runs.

81
00:05:36,040 --> 00:05:39,220
He was selling supplies to the miners.

82
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:44,720
Always, I think, he was thinking about how he could make buck.

83
00:05:44,721 --> 00:05:50,560
Clark used part of his $2,000 in gold to buy a wagon.

84
00:05:51,240 --> 00:05:55,680
Then he set off across the mountains for Salt Lake City, 400 miles away.

85
00:05:56,540 --> 00:06:03,280
Two months later, in the dead of winter, he returned to Montana with a wagon load of eggs, all of them frozen solid.

86
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:09,620
Clark sold them to hungry miners for $3 a dozen.

87
00:06:10,900 --> 00:06:14,520
As long as the profit bottom line was good, the people be damned.

88
00:06:14,720 --> 00:06:17,840
He would take whatever the market would bear,

89
00:06:17,841 --> 00:06:21,800
and, you know, if a miner had to work all day for a dollar

90
00:06:21,801 --> 00:06:26,020
and he charged him three dollars for eggs, he didn't really care.

91
00:06:26,400 --> 00:06:30,980
Those early merchants in the 1860s and 1870s

92
00:06:30,981 --> 00:06:36,040
really earned great money hauling in those precious cargoes

93
00:06:36,041 --> 00:06:39,580
like salt and produce and fruit and vegetables.

94
00:06:40,040 --> 00:06:44,240
A pound of salt could go as high as $150 in those early days.

95
00:06:44,720 --> 00:06:49,080
With prices like those, merchant Clark swiftly amassed a small fortune.

96
00:06:50,100 --> 00:06:53,780
In 1872, Clark used his profits from eggs and salt

97
00:06:53,781 --> 00:06:57,120
to open a bank in the supply town called Deer Lodge.

98
00:06:57,960 --> 00:07:02,960
Now, besides gouging the miners for supplies, he could gouge them for loans too.

99
00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:05,880
He was kind of a small man.

100
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:10,460
Maybe he had that small man problem that some men have.

101
00:07:10,500 --> 00:07:11,200
I don't know.

102
00:07:11,201 --> 00:07:19,240
I think he was kind of dapper in his clothes and fancied himself a bon vivant around town.

103
00:07:19,880 --> 00:07:24,020
But while Clark was getting rich, Montana's miners were going bust.

104
00:07:24,640 --> 00:07:29,540
By the late 1860s, the gold was giving out and miners were giving up.

105
00:07:30,420 --> 00:07:33,540
Clark began snapping up mines for pennies on the dollar.

106
00:07:34,640 --> 00:07:36,720
His timing couldn't have been better.

107
00:07:37,680 --> 00:07:41,240
Gold wasn't the only precious metal buried in the Montana hills.

108
00:07:42,160 --> 00:07:45,220
On a hillside above Silver Bow Creek in Butte,

109
00:07:45,660 --> 00:07:50,820
a persistent miner named William Farland had spied an unusual outcropping of granite.

110
00:07:51,460 --> 00:07:54,800
Farland suspected that granite might be hiding something else.

111
00:07:55,500 --> 00:08:00,980
Farland was one of the first people to take the outcroppings to an assayer.

112
00:08:01,580 --> 00:08:04,980
And ultimately, when it broke down, it proved to be very rich in silver.

113
00:08:05,840 --> 00:08:11,440
Farland named his new silver mine the Travona, and it proved chock full of silver.

114
00:08:12,180 --> 00:08:18,400
But extricating the precious metal required facilities that didn't exist in Butte or anywhere within hundreds of miles.

115
00:08:19,520 --> 00:08:22,980
Farland needed an ore mill to crush his ore and get the silver out.

116
00:08:23,380 --> 00:08:25,980
And he needed a loan to build that mill.

117
00:08:26,920 --> 00:08:30,040
Hat in hand, Farland paid a call on a local banker.

118
00:08:30,700 --> 00:08:32,940
The banker, of course, was William Clark.

119
00:08:32,941 --> 00:08:37,996
He went to a man he'd met in the gold fields, W.A.

120
00:08:37,997 --> 00:08:41,940
Clark, and went and borrowed money from

121
00:08:41,941 --> 00:08:47,025
the Clark and Larrabee Bank down in Deer Lodge to get started.

122
00:08:47,026 --> 00:08:49,240
Clark knew that Farland was

123
00:08:49,241 --> 00:08:55,046
a good miner. Clark agreed to the loan, but demanded the Travona as collateral.

124
00:08:55,047 --> 00:08:55,747
Farland

125
00:08:55,748 --> 00:08:58,092
hesitated. Then Clark made an offer.

126
00:08:58,093 --> 00:09:01,820
He would hire Farland to mill ore from one of Clark's

127
00:09:01,821 --> 00:09:07,169
minds. With that money, Farland could repay the loan. Farland signed.

128
00:09:07,170 --> 00:09:09,340
He figured he couldn't lose.

129
00:09:10,140 --> 00:09:12,675
He couldn't have been more wrong.

130
00:09:12,676 --> 00:09:17,900
Clark never sent his ore to the mill, and Farland soon found himself

131
00:09:17,901 --> 00:09:21,958
behind on his loan payments. He asked Clark for an extension.

132
00:09:21,959 --> 00:09:24,220
Clark, of course, turned him down.

133
00:09:24,960 --> 00:09:29,100
When Farland asked him for a extension in the loan, of course, it was sure.

134
00:09:29,101 --> 00:09:32,400
Farland did not pay back the banker.

135
00:09:33,040 --> 00:09:35,080
And when you don't pay the banker back,

136
00:09:35,180 --> 00:09:38,360
they usually kind of end up with whatever you put up for collateral.

137
00:09:39,100 --> 00:09:43,420
W.A. Clark would pounce when he saw the opportunity to make money.

138
00:09:43,421 --> 00:09:48,100
He didn't think twice about foreclosing on Farland.

139
00:09:49,040 --> 00:09:50,600
In one cold-blooded move,

140
00:09:51,120 --> 00:09:53,960
William Clark now owned Butte's best silver mine

141
00:09:53,961 --> 00:09:55,980
and the only mill in town.

142
00:09:55,981 --> 00:10:03,500
From his humble beginnings panning for gold flakes, he was becoming one of the richest and most powerful men in the West.

143
00:10:04,060 --> 00:10:06,440
And Butte was his undisputed kingdom.

144
00:10:07,260 --> 00:10:09,740
But all that was about to change.

145
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:17,760
When we return, a new miner and a new metal show up in Butte, and the battle for the richest hill on Earth begins.

146
00:10:19,460 --> 00:10:23,640
Copper has a history of use that is at least 10,000 years old.

147
00:10:23,641 --> 00:10:29,880
A copper pendant discovered in what is now northern Iraq goes back to about 8700 B.C.

148
00:10:30,460 --> 00:10:32,340
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

149
00:10:34,200 --> 00:10:37,780
We now return to Copper Kings on Modern Marvels.

150
00:10:41,440 --> 00:10:48,320
By the 1870s, the mountain mines of the American West were pouring forth rivers of gold and silver.

151
00:10:49,180 --> 00:10:52,600
The Colorado gold fields proved even richer than California's.

152
00:10:52,601 --> 00:10:57,780
and Nevada's Comstock load contained a king's ransom of both gold and silver.

153
00:10:59,260 --> 00:11:03,000
All it took to get rich was a knack for knowing where to dig.

154
00:11:03,540 --> 00:11:06,500
But not many miners actually had that knack.

155
00:11:07,440 --> 00:11:10,000
One man who did was Marcus Daly.

156
00:11:11,120 --> 00:11:16,360
Daly was an Irishman, one of thousands who fled starvation during the Irish potato famine.

157
00:11:17,120 --> 00:11:23,320
When he got off the boat in New York in 1856, he was 15, poor, and uneducated.

158
00:11:26,100 --> 00:11:30,400
Daly, all through his life, bore the marks of his peasant background.

159
00:11:30,580 --> 00:11:31,460
He was stooped.

160
00:11:32,040 --> 00:11:35,520
He had great trouble with the language, both speaking it and writing it.

161
00:11:35,920 --> 00:11:38,700
Caused some people to dismiss him as being slow-witted.

162
00:11:39,260 --> 00:11:40,880
Actually, he was very intelligent.

163
00:11:40,881 --> 00:11:46,629
it. Unlike William Clark, who hated hard labor, Daley embraced it.

164
00:11:46,630 --> 00:11:48,720
He worked for five years

165
00:11:48,721 --> 00:11:52,982
in New York as a dock hand, a stable hand, and a leather worker.

166
00:11:52,983 --> 00:11:54,380
He scrimped and saved

167
00:11:54,381 --> 00:11:58,148
to buy passage by steamer to San Francisco.

168
00:11:58,149 --> 00:12:01,740
In the mines of California, his first job

169
00:12:01,741 --> 00:12:04,717
was hauling buckets of water to the toiling miners.

170
00:12:04,718 --> 00:12:06,760
As they talked with the hardworking

171
00:12:06,761 --> 00:12:10,950
young immigrant, the other miners soon noticed something different about Daley.

172
00:12:10,951 --> 00:12:11,651
He could tell

173
00:12:11,652 --> 00:12:14,892
somehow where they should dig to hit pay dirt.

174
00:12:14,893 --> 00:12:17,720
Daley, it seemed, had a nose for mining.

175
00:12:19,740 --> 00:12:25,240
We believe here in mining places that mining people are born, they're not made.

176
00:12:25,600 --> 00:12:30,020
Marcus Daley was a true miner. He understood mining.

177
00:12:30,021 --> 00:12:37,200
In 1862, Daly left California for Nevada and the Comstock Mines.

178
00:12:38,080 --> 00:12:42,640
In the booming hills around Virginia City, Daly teamed up with George Hurst,

179
00:12:42,960 --> 00:12:47,220
a miner who had struggled in California, but was finally striking it rich.

180
00:12:48,140 --> 00:12:51,440
The Hurst family would later become famous for its publishing empire.

181
00:12:51,960 --> 00:12:55,820
But in the 1860s, George Hurst was still a mining man.

182
00:12:57,340 --> 00:12:59,360
Well, Hurst and Daly were quite a lot alike.

183
00:12:59,361 --> 00:13:01,640
I mean, Hurst was like Daley.

184
00:13:01,820 --> 00:13:04,560
You could find Hurst or Daley sitting on a curbstone,

185
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,200
talking with their men, sharing a chew of tobacco.

186
00:13:08,160 --> 00:13:11,680
Hurst was notoriously unkempt, called Uncle George.

187
00:13:12,240 --> 00:13:14,480
He made fortunes and lost fortunes.

188
00:13:14,880 --> 00:13:18,440
Both of them really made their big start on the Comstock load in Nevada.

189
00:13:19,220 --> 00:13:21,860
In the hills outside Salt Lake City, Utah,

190
00:13:22,380 --> 00:13:25,660
Daley steered Hurst to a silver claim called the Ontario.

191
00:13:26,220 --> 00:13:29,340
Hurst bought it and put Daley in charge of developing it.

192
00:13:29,360 --> 00:13:36,360
Under Daly's management, the Ontario soon became the richest silver mine in the world.

193
00:13:36,361 --> 00:13:41,360
He made Hearst a great deal of money by nosing out mining properties,

194
00:13:41,361 --> 00:13:48,360
by being able to explain how to purchase mining properties

195
00:13:48,361 --> 00:13:54,360
and how to tell whether they were going to hold the mother load or not.

196
00:13:54,361 --> 00:13:58,360
The Ontario made George Hearst a multi-millionaire.

197
00:13:58,361 --> 00:14:01,360
It made Marcus Daly a legend among miners.

198
00:14:01,361 --> 00:14:07,360
It used to be said of Daly, he could see into the ground farther than any other man that anyone knew.

199
00:14:07,361 --> 00:14:12,360
And it was that that really was the genesis, as I understand it, of the Hurst fortune.

200
00:14:12,361 --> 00:14:18,360
Hurst had made money before, but this silver mine at Ontario was the big break.

201
00:14:18,361 --> 00:14:24,360
In 1876, the same year William Clark foreclosed on the Travona silver mine and its ore mill,

202
00:14:24,361 --> 00:14:27,360
Marcus Daly came to Butte.

203
00:14:27,361 --> 00:14:32,060
As usual, Daly was looking for promising mining claims.

204
00:14:33,020 --> 00:14:35,800
This time, though, he wasn't working for George Hurst.

205
00:14:36,660 --> 00:14:41,260
Daly had been hired by the Walker Brothers, wealthy bankers from Salt Lake City.

206
00:14:42,060 --> 00:14:46,680
His assignment was to check out a Butte silver deposit called the Rainbow Load.

207
00:14:48,760 --> 00:14:51,440
Daly's arrival caused quite a stir in Butte.

208
00:14:51,441 --> 00:14:58,720
Everybody knew this was a prestigious mine appraiser, investor, and manager.

209
00:14:58,880 --> 00:15:04,940
So when Daly comes to town representing the wealthy Walker bankers from Salt Lake City, Butte is very glad to see him.

210
00:15:05,760 --> 00:15:10,220
Not everyone in Butte was glad to see Daly, particularly William Clark.

211
00:15:11,160 --> 00:15:15,620
The Butte community worshipped Daly because he represented big money from the outside.

212
00:15:16,180 --> 00:15:19,100
My guess is that Clark didn't particularly care for Daley

213
00:15:19,101 --> 00:15:22,120
because of his immigrant Catholic background,

214
00:15:22,480 --> 00:15:25,528
but probably more so because he was getting more of the limelight than Mr.

215
00:15:25,529 --> 00:15:26,229
Clark was.

216
00:15:27,360 --> 00:15:31,600
At Daley's urging, the Walker brothers snapped up a mine on the Rainbow Load

217
00:15:31,601 --> 00:15:36,240
called the Alice Mine, a mine that Clark himself had been eyeing.

218
00:15:37,440 --> 00:15:41,960
The Walkers also gave Daley a small share of the mine and asked him to run it.

219
00:15:41,961 --> 00:15:45,460
William Clark was furious.

220
00:15:45,461 --> 00:15:49,660
He wasted no time letting Daley know he was unwelcome in Butte.

221
00:15:49,661 --> 00:15:53,560
Clark owned a claim directly uphill from the Alice.

222
00:15:53,561 --> 00:15:57,860
He ordered his miners to dig tunnels right alongside Daley's, but

223
00:15:57,861 --> 00:16:04,260
a few strategic feet higher. Clark left his mine higher,

224
00:16:04,261 --> 00:16:07,900
his working areas of his mine higher than that of the

225
00:16:07,901 --> 00:16:14,220
Alice so that the waters from his properties would drain into the Alice mine anyway and they'd

226
00:16:14,221 --> 00:16:19,340
be responsible for pumping them and increasing the cost of their production in that area.

227
00:16:20,220 --> 00:16:24,839
Soon water was pouring down from Clark's tunnels and flooding Daley's.

228
00:16:24,840 --> 00:16:26,620
But Daley wasn't one to let

229
00:16:26,621 --> 00:16:28,289
a little sabotage stop him.

230
00:16:28,290 --> 00:16:32,740
He installed massive pumps to drain the water and kept right on shipping

231
00:16:32,741 --> 00:16:41,460
silver to the walkers walker brothers are processing silver worth millions it's been coming out pure

232
00:16:41,461 --> 00:16:48,740
silver bars practically from the ground and these guys are making millions of dollars with very

233
00:16:48,741 --> 00:16:58,020
little investment the legend of daly's golden touch or in this case silver touch grew even stronger

234
00:16:58,021 --> 00:17:02,340
But so did William Clark's resolve to drive him from Butte.

235
00:17:02,940 --> 00:17:06,520
What happened next, though, would keep Daley in Montana forever.

236
00:17:09,280 --> 00:17:13,660
Tucked into an unusual outcropping of black granite high on Butte Hill

237
00:17:13,661 --> 00:17:16,940
was a small silver mine called the Anaconda.

238
00:17:17,840 --> 00:17:20,700
Daley's sixth sense told him it was something special.

239
00:17:21,760 --> 00:17:23,680
He has a nose for mining.

240
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:25,900
He knows mining in his bones.

241
00:17:25,901 --> 00:17:31,000
He's been telling the big boys where to buy their mining property for a long time.

242
00:17:31,001 --> 00:17:35,300
So when he sees this anaconda mine, he knows it's good.

243
00:17:35,301 --> 00:17:39,800
Supposedly, it was almost an afterthought that he went and looked at the anaconda mine and bought it.

244
00:17:39,801 --> 00:17:43,200
We honestly do not know whether he took it to the walkers.

245
00:17:43,201 --> 00:17:49,700
My guess is that he didn't because he certainly broke with them and they resented him, hated him after that day.

246
00:17:49,701 --> 00:17:55,200
Daly bought the anaconda and a neighboring claim for $30,000.

247
00:17:55,201 --> 00:18:02,200
After years of running other men's mines, Daley finally owned a mine of his own.

248
00:18:02,201 --> 00:18:08,200
Unfortunately, he'd spent every cent he had just buying the Anaconda.

249
00:18:08,201 --> 00:18:11,200
The expensive part was yet to come.

250
00:18:11,201 --> 00:18:14,200
The investment in the mine is only the beginning.

251
00:18:14,201 --> 00:18:17,200
One needs capital for a stamping mill, for smelting,

252
00:18:17,201 --> 00:18:21,200
whether you build your own smelter or have it custom-smelted for you.

253
00:18:21,201 --> 00:18:24,200
So Daley did not have that kind of money.

254
00:18:24,201 --> 00:18:28,960
But Daly knew someone who did, his old pal George Hurst.

255
00:18:30,280 --> 00:18:32,340
Daly had made millions for Hurst.

256
00:18:32,680 --> 00:18:34,860
Surely Hurst could spare a bit of that fortune.

257
00:18:36,960 --> 00:18:38,880
Daly went to Hurst and asked for backing.

258
00:18:39,720 --> 00:18:43,980
With a little bit of investment, Daly promised, the Anaconda could pay off big.

259
00:18:45,760 --> 00:18:49,980
But Daly wasn't the only one who came calling on Hurst to discuss the Anaconda.

260
00:18:49,981 --> 00:18:55,040
hot on the heels of Marcus Daly came William Clark

261
00:18:55,041 --> 00:19:01,760
Clark knew that Daly would be his biggest competitor and that he if he could stop him

262
00:19:01,761 --> 00:19:08,300
there he would have it all so Clark tried to undermine Daly at that point and told Hurst

263
00:19:08,301 --> 00:19:13,860
Daly was not the man he thought he was that he wouldn't be able to develop the mind that he

264
00:19:13,861 --> 00:19:19,560
wasn't as good as he hurt as he was presenting himself but Hurst had evidence to the contrary

265
00:19:19,561 --> 00:19:21,700
millions of dollars worth of evidence.

266
00:19:22,480 --> 00:19:25,820
Hearst hadn't forgotten that it was Daley who found him his fortune.

267
00:19:26,700 --> 00:19:33,640
Marcus Daley and George Hearst are two men who understand mining.

268
00:19:34,280 --> 00:19:37,200
And I think they had a lot of respect for each other.

269
00:19:37,740 --> 00:19:44,180
And I think when Clark came, maybe Hearst questioned a little bit of what was going on.

270
00:19:45,140 --> 00:19:49,020
Hearst showed William Clark the door and wrote Daley a check.

271
00:19:49,560 --> 00:19:52,200
Daly no longer owned the Anaconda outright.

272
00:19:52,680 --> 00:19:55,420
Hurst and his partners now owned a sizable share of the mine.

273
00:19:55,980 --> 00:20:00,300
But now Daly could tap the deep pockets of one of America's great fortunes.

274
00:20:00,980 --> 00:20:03,740
Money in hand, Daly began to dig.

275
00:20:04,300 --> 00:20:10,120
But it soon started to look like Daly's legendary nose for riches might be failing him.

276
00:20:11,420 --> 00:20:17,320
The first copper deposit worked extensively in America is located in Granby, Connecticut.

277
00:20:17,321 --> 00:20:21,360
It was operated from 1705 until 1770.

278
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:23,800
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

279
00:20:25,260 --> 00:20:28,320
We now return to Copper Kings on Modern Marvels.

280
00:20:38,580 --> 00:20:43,180
By 1880, with the engine of American prosperity gathering steam,

281
00:20:43,181 --> 00:20:47,860
the mining rivalry between Marcus Daly and William Clark was heating up.

282
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:54,220
Daly had bet everything he had on the Anaconda mine.

283
00:20:54,800 --> 00:20:59,100
He'd also persuaded millionaire George Hurst to bankroll equipment and a workforce.

284
00:20:59,880 --> 00:21:03,880
But the deeper Daly's men dug, the less silver they found.

285
00:21:05,380 --> 00:21:10,260
Once they started running out of the silver ore, the mine almost looked worthless.

286
00:21:10,261 --> 00:21:17,680
As you come to the end of a vein that's an economic vein, you have a couple of choices.

287
00:21:17,980 --> 00:21:25,100
Obviously, you can go out of business, or you can start looking around to see if you can find some more of the similar type of ore.

288
00:21:25,300 --> 00:21:31,400
But as you come to the end of the veins or the economic ore, you're going to go broke if you keep going.

289
00:21:31,580 --> 00:21:35,080
So you start getting a little bit panicked about what you're going to do.

290
00:21:35,081 --> 00:21:41,080
Clark watched with smug satisfaction as Daley poured more and more money down

291
00:21:41,081 --> 00:21:46,580
the empty mine shaft. The rumor was that they sank four million dollars, a huge

292
00:21:46,581 --> 00:21:50,640
amount, in those days into the anaconda before they ever took a penny out of it.

293
00:21:50,641 --> 00:21:56,360
But Daley refused to give Clark the satisfaction of giving up. Daley told his

294
00:21:56,361 --> 00:22:08,167
men to keep digging and dig they did. 100 feet, 150, 200, 250, 300 feet down.

295
00:22:08,168 --> 00:22:10,160
Daly's miners

296
00:22:10,161 --> 00:22:14,807
finally struck something, but it wasn't silver. It was copper.

297
00:22:14,808 --> 00:22:17,280
They were down there blasting and

298
00:22:17,281 --> 00:22:21,017
some of his miners came up and said, there's some new material here.

299
00:22:21,018 --> 00:22:22,720
But Daly knew what it was. He'd

300
00:22:22,721 --> 00:22:28,780
seen copper before, and the minute he saw that, he realized that they had hit a massive vein of

301
00:22:28,781 --> 00:22:30,460
mid-range copper ore.

302
00:22:30,461 --> 00:22:36,540
The deeper they went, the less the silver and the higher the copper. He gets

303
00:22:36,541 --> 00:22:45,380
into an ore body of copper sulfide that is so pure and so rich that to date there had never been that

304
00:22:45,381 --> 00:22:48,226
kind of a find in any mine in America.

305
00:22:48,227 --> 00:22:52,120
Of course, back then, no one was looking for copper.

306
00:22:52,720 --> 00:22:56,660
Copper was the stuff of church roofs, brass buttons, pots and pans.

307
00:22:57,280 --> 00:22:59,080
Silver and gold were where the money was.

308
00:22:59,600 --> 00:23:01,740
But Daly's timing was perfect.

309
00:23:02,640 --> 00:23:06,620
And at the exact same time, the United States of America

310
00:23:06,621 --> 00:23:10,720
is starting to take interest in a guy named Edison,

311
00:23:10,900 --> 00:23:12,220
who has invented a light bulb.

312
00:23:13,160 --> 00:23:20,300
And so what you have going on here is telegraph, telephone, electricity.

313
00:23:20,301 --> 00:23:26,680
and all of a sudden there's this need for a conductor of this electricity

314
00:23:26,681 --> 00:23:32,000
and they find gold is too expensive, silver isn't quite as good,

315
00:23:32,001 --> 00:23:36,340
but copper, golly, that's the metal they need.

316
00:23:36,940 --> 00:23:39,640
Suddenly, America couldn't get enough copper.

317
00:23:41,200 --> 00:23:45,840
Utility companies, phone companies and wire manufacturers clamored for copper

318
00:23:45,841 --> 00:23:48,900
and Marcus Daly had more than anyone else.

319
00:23:50,300 --> 00:23:56,760
The Anaconda Mine became the Anaconda Company, a fast-growing empire sprawling across Butte Hill.

320
00:23:57,520 --> 00:24:02,000
Hundreds of miners worked day and night, burrowing deep into the mountain of copper.

321
00:24:04,220 --> 00:24:09,000
Surprisingly, while demand increased, copper prices actually dropped.

322
00:24:09,900 --> 00:24:13,060
Michigan copper mines, America's previous leader in production,

323
00:24:13,540 --> 00:24:18,840
cut prices to compete with Montana from 20 cents a pound to just 10 cents a pound.

324
00:24:20,300 --> 00:24:23,300
but Montana's mines were too big to beat now.

325
00:24:26,300 --> 00:24:28,300
Butte's population skyrocketed.

326
00:24:28,301 --> 00:24:32,300
With Daly and Clark competing for workers, wages were good.

327
00:24:32,301 --> 00:24:36,300
Nearly three dollars a day, the best mining pay in America.

328
00:24:36,301 --> 00:24:39,300
True, the work was difficult and dangerous.

329
00:24:39,301 --> 00:24:42,300
A Butte miner's life expectancy was just 38 years.

330
00:24:42,301 --> 00:24:45,300
The deep mines were very dangerous places.

331
00:24:45,301 --> 00:24:48,300
The dangers were of lung-based diseases,

332
00:24:48,301 --> 00:24:54,260
silicosis, inhaling dust laden with metal from the mines, tuberculosis,

333
00:24:54,660 --> 00:24:58,040
and of course the ever-present danger of terrible mine disasters,

334
00:24:58,280 --> 00:25:01,740
whether they'd be fires or explosions or cave-ins.

335
00:25:02,260 --> 00:25:04,260
But the pay was irresistible.

336
00:25:05,080 --> 00:25:09,920
Miners flocked to Butte by the thousands from all over America and throughout Europe too.

337
00:25:10,460 --> 00:25:13,940
Englishmen, Irishmen, people from Wales.

338
00:25:13,941 --> 00:25:20,760
There were Scandinavian, Swedish, Norwegian, and they were hoping to find the American dream.

339
00:25:21,620 --> 00:25:28,800
The copper boom blew Butte wide open, transforming it from a sleepy mining camp to a full-fledged industrial city.

340
00:25:29,980 --> 00:25:35,200
It was brimming with miners who worked hard and drank even harder.

341
00:25:36,500 --> 00:25:38,120
We had had some of the epic bars.

342
00:25:38,420 --> 00:25:41,740
The Atlantic Bar was a city block long, went 24 hours a day.

343
00:25:41,741 --> 00:25:48,540
And if you kept drinking, there was all the pork and the eggs and everything that you would like to eat.

344
00:25:49,020 --> 00:25:57,421
They celebrated the fact that they never closed, often by simply taking the key on opening day and dropping it down the toilet to show the doors here would never be

345
00:25:57,422 --> 00:25:58,122
locked.

346
00:25:58,440 --> 00:26:00,640
The good times weren't limited to the bars.

347
00:26:01,700 --> 00:26:06,520
One of the biggest, wildest red light districts in America sprang up in Butte.

348
00:26:07,480 --> 00:26:13,040
One house of prostitution called the bullpen that supposedly worked hundreds of prostitutes.

349
00:26:13,400 --> 00:26:17,860
It roared night and day, three eight-hour shifts endlessly rotating.

350
00:26:18,540 --> 00:26:22,620
So when one shift was underground, the other would be above ground playing.

351
00:26:22,820 --> 00:26:29,400
The owners of the mining companies were very interested in keeping the miners happy.

352
00:26:30,480 --> 00:26:33,260
They didn't necessarily want them to unionize,

353
00:26:33,261 --> 00:26:37,900
and they felt that if the miners had sex and they had alcohol,

354
00:26:38,500 --> 00:26:41,940
that this would keep them, I mean, this first of all would keep them impoverished

355
00:26:41,941 --> 00:26:46,300
because every time they got a paycheck, they'd spend it at the whorehouse or at the saloon.

356
00:26:46,440 --> 00:26:50,520
So their whole paycheck went back into the community.

357
00:26:51,700 --> 00:26:54,900
And in many cases into William Clark's pockets.

358
00:26:55,540 --> 00:26:58,480
As Butte grew, so did the value of Clark's claims.

359
00:26:58,720 --> 00:27:02,800
Through a holding company called the Colorado and Montana Company,

360
00:27:02,801 --> 00:27:06,280
plus a growing network of mines and processing facilities,

361
00:27:06,800 --> 00:27:09,700
Clark began tapping his silver mines for copper, too.

362
00:27:10,420 --> 00:27:14,160
As Clark and Daly's production soared, so did their fortunes.

363
00:27:14,800 --> 00:27:18,860
But those fortunes did nothing to distract them from their bitter rivalry.

364
00:27:19,800 --> 00:27:22,820
Daly hadn't forgotten Clark's scheme to flood the Alice mine

365
00:27:22,821 --> 00:27:25,820
or his effort to turn George Hurst against him.

366
00:27:26,740 --> 00:27:28,380
In the spring of 1883,

367
00:27:28,700 --> 00:27:32,360
Daly summoned George Hurst and his other financial partners to Butte.

368
00:27:32,800 --> 00:27:39,620
Daly had a plan to turn the Anaconda Company into a colossus that would leave Clark far behind.

369
00:27:40,980 --> 00:27:45,920
Daly in 1883 brought them to the top of the Butte Hill from California,

370
00:27:45,921 --> 00:27:51,240
and he explained a rather very outlandish proposal to them,

371
00:27:51,740 --> 00:27:56,260
and that was that they go 26 miles to the west of Butte

372
00:27:56,261 --> 00:28:01,580
to build the world's largest copper smelter in that particular area,

373
00:28:01,581 --> 00:28:06,020
and eventually built a railroad to connect the two communities.

374
00:28:07,300 --> 00:28:10,720
Bailey wanted to erect not only the world's largest copper smelter,

375
00:28:10,980 --> 00:28:13,340
but an entire city built from the ground up.

376
00:28:14,980 --> 00:28:19,540
The city's sole purpose would be to keep the huge smelter running day and night.

377
00:28:22,880 --> 00:28:26,180
Bailey had it all figured out right down to the city's name.

378
00:28:26,181 --> 00:28:28,380
He would call it Anaconda.

379
00:28:31,580 --> 00:28:35,900
Hurst and his partners told Daley he could count on them for whatever he needed.

380
00:28:37,760 --> 00:28:41,320
Soon, an army of workers swarmed across the valley floor.

381
00:28:42,600 --> 00:28:44,180
They built hundreds of houses.

382
00:28:44,720 --> 00:28:48,120
They cut millions of board feet of timber to build the mighty smelter.

383
00:28:49,420 --> 00:28:53,220
In just one year, Daley's town and smelter became a reality.

384
00:28:54,040 --> 00:28:59,340
Later, he would lay a 26-mile rail line to feed more and more ore to the smelter.

385
00:28:59,341 --> 00:29:13,341
When you look at what Daly did, he built a smelter, he built the community of Anaconda, he built a water system to feed the smelter, he built a railroad from Butte to

386
00:29:14,198 --> 00:29:19,300
Anaconda to take the ore to the smelter. It's incredible.

387
00:29:19,301 --> 00:29:28,380
In 1887, Daly and the Anaconda turned out 57 million pounds of copper, more than three times Clark's total.

388
00:29:29,080 --> 00:29:30,980
But Daly's triumph was short-lived.

389
00:29:31,700 --> 00:29:33,360
Months later, disaster struck.

390
00:29:33,920 --> 00:29:40,180
The immense wooden smelter complex, sprawling across an area the size of several city blocks, caught fire.

391
00:29:41,020 --> 00:29:45,440
As the townspeople watched in horror, the vast smelter burned to the ground.

392
00:29:46,340 --> 00:29:51,720
The fire looked suspicious, and many fingers pointed in the direction of Daly's rival, William Clark.

393
00:29:54,640 --> 00:30:01,440
When we return, Daly stands amid the ruins, and Clark aims to complete his victory.

394
00:30:03,680 --> 00:30:07,140
Since bacteria will not grow on a copper surface,

395
00:30:07,141 --> 00:30:11,620
many hospitals use copper doorknobs to reduce the transfer of disease.

396
00:30:12,180 --> 00:30:13,980
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

397
00:30:13,981 --> 00:30:18,540
We now return to Copper Kings on Modern Marbles.

398
00:30:21,000 --> 00:30:28,980
By the late 1880s, America's second industrial revolution was going strong, powered not by steam, but by electricity.

399
00:30:29,960 --> 00:30:34,940
Much of the electricity ran on copper from the Montana mines of Marcus Daly and William Clark.

400
00:30:34,941 --> 00:30:42,940
A half-mile underground, legions of miners toiled for a few dollars a day to keep the

401
00:30:42,941 --> 00:30:46,080
metal flowing for the rival tycoons.

402
00:30:46,081 --> 00:30:50,640
What made it so valuable now was as a conductor of electricity and for the communications

403
00:30:50,641 --> 00:30:51,640
industry.

404
00:30:51,641 --> 00:30:56,520
So it's the spreading use of electricity, which is mushrooming in the 80s and 90s, and

405
00:30:56,521 --> 00:31:01,960
of the telephone industry at the same time, that now have massive new uses for copper.

406
00:31:01,961 --> 00:31:08,260
The value of the metal is zooming, and America is the world leader in copper during these years.

407
00:31:08,261 --> 00:31:15,400
And Butte quickly surpasses Michigan to become the leading American producer of copper.

408
00:31:16,120 --> 00:31:23,200
Self-made Irish immigrant Marcus Daly had scored a coup in 1884, firing up the world's largest copper smelter.

409
00:31:24,020 --> 00:31:27,740
But in 1888, Daly's smelter lay in charred ruins.

410
00:31:27,741 --> 00:31:32,400
the entire facility had mysteriously caught fire and burned to the ground.

411
00:31:33,260 --> 00:31:36,620
Suspicion fell on William Clark, but there was no proof.

412
00:31:38,340 --> 00:31:43,400
A weaker man might have given up, but to Marcus Daly, that was unthinkable.

413
00:31:44,220 --> 00:31:46,420
Daly ordered his men to rebuild the smelter.

414
00:31:46,840 --> 00:31:49,020
This time, he made it fireproof.

415
00:31:52,300 --> 00:31:54,600
One year after the disastrous fire,

416
00:31:54,601 --> 00:32:01,120
the anaconda smelter, bigger and better than ever, was once more the world's leading source of copper.

417
00:32:02,760 --> 00:32:07,820
80 miles west of anaconda, in Hamilton, far from the smoky smelter stacks,

418
00:32:08,400 --> 00:32:11,800
Daly and his wife began building a 20,000 acre estate.

419
00:32:12,840 --> 00:32:16,060
The Daly's and their four children moved in in 1889,

420
00:32:16,420 --> 00:32:22,260
but moved out temporarily in 1897 to remodel the home to make it more conducive to entertaining.

421
00:32:22,261 --> 00:32:27,260
Daly named the mansion and grounds Bitter Root Farms.

422
00:32:27,261 --> 00:32:32,260
But Daly's rival, William Clark, wasn't exactly living in squalor either.

423
00:32:32,261 --> 00:32:37,260
Clark occupied a lavish mansion of his own high on the side of Butte Hill.

424
00:32:37,261 --> 00:32:42,260
His wife, Clark's childhood sweetheart, Catherine, was hardly ever there, though.

425
00:32:42,261 --> 00:32:49,260
She and Clark's children preferred the salons of Europe to the rough and tumble environs of Montana.

426
00:32:49,261 --> 00:32:58,460
Both Clark and Daly lived like kings, modeling their lives after the example of robber barons like the Vanderbilts and the Morgans.

427
00:32:59,060 --> 00:33:02,197
Clark spent $260,000 building this house.

428
00:33:02,198 --> 00:33:06,100
That was an enormous amount of money in those days.

429
00:33:06,900 --> 00:33:10,520
He invited European artists and craftsmen to come and work on the house.

430
00:33:10,521 --> 00:33:15,660
Many of the ceilings you'll notice are hand-painted Presco ceilings.

431
00:33:16,220 --> 00:33:26,120
The beautiful glass windows in the mansion, one just above me, is 17 feet tall, all made of stained glass.

432
00:33:26,340 --> 00:33:27,480
An amazing thing.

433
00:33:29,000 --> 00:33:34,120
Comfortably ensconced in their mansions, the two rivals might have chosen to make peace with each other.

434
00:33:34,660 --> 00:33:38,840
Instead, their feud was about to explode into all-out war.

435
00:33:40,520 --> 00:33:47,760
In 1888, the same year Daley Smelter burned to the ground, the people of Montana were

436
00:33:47,761 --> 00:33:51,780
about to elect their first delegate to the U.S. Congress.

437
00:33:51,781 --> 00:33:56,620
And in William Clark's humble opinion, there was only one man for the job, the Democratic

438
00:33:56,621 --> 00:34:01,180
candidate, William Clark.

439
00:34:01,181 --> 00:34:06,000
Daley like Clark was a Democrat too, but he immediately threw his wealth and power behind

440
00:34:06,001 --> 00:34:07,540
Clark's Republican opponent.

441
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:15,120
His sudden conversion to Republicanism wasn't due solely to his rivalry with Clark, though.

442
00:34:17,320 --> 00:34:21,740
Daly's men had illegally cut thousands of acres of government-owned timber.

443
00:34:22,560 --> 00:34:23,900
The government was suing.

444
00:34:25,160 --> 00:34:28,500
Daly was hoping a sympathetic congressman might get him off the hook.

445
00:34:29,880 --> 00:34:33,760
Daly and his co-investors were eager to have a Republican

446
00:34:33,761 --> 00:34:37,740
representing them hopefully to a Republican administration in Washington.

447
00:34:38,220 --> 00:34:42,100
So it would appear that Daley and his allies undercut Clark,

448
00:34:42,560 --> 00:34:44,280
even though they were all Democrats,

449
00:34:44,800 --> 00:34:48,840
mainly for hard practical business reasons.

450
00:34:49,940 --> 00:34:52,800
Daley didn't stop with merely endorsing Clark's opponent.

451
00:34:53,860 --> 00:34:55,900
When Election Day rolled around,

452
00:34:56,300 --> 00:34:59,860
Daley's foremen collected ballots from thousands of Anaconda workers.

453
00:35:00,580 --> 00:35:05,040
Then Daley's men inspected the ballots to make sure they were filled out correctly.

454
00:35:05,920 --> 00:35:08,320
Clark lost by 5,000 votes.

455
00:35:09,260 --> 00:35:14,360
Clark never forgave or forgot what Daley and his associates did to him in 1888,

456
00:35:14,460 --> 00:35:17,080
and he was determined to get revenge in any way he could.

457
00:35:18,240 --> 00:35:20,540
Five years later, Clark ran for the Senate.

458
00:35:21,340 --> 00:35:23,520
Daley stopped him the same way he had before.

459
00:35:24,220 --> 00:35:28,460
Clark vowed to get even, and soon he saw a golden opportunity.

460
00:35:29,860 --> 00:35:37,320
In 1894, Marcus Daly launched a campaign to move Montana's state capital from Helena to Anaconda.

461
00:35:38,880 --> 00:35:43,280
Clark threw all of his weight and much of his money behind Helena.

462
00:35:44,500 --> 00:35:53,900
Daly wanted Anaconda, I think, for the very simple, sentimental reason that it was his town, his company town, so to speak.

463
00:35:54,240 --> 00:35:56,820
Clark made fun of Daly's town.

464
00:35:56,821 --> 00:35:59,680
he made fun of the fact that it was a blue-collar town

465
00:35:59,681 --> 00:36:01,720
and the people weren't as sophisticated.

466
00:36:03,000 --> 00:36:06,080
Clark's forces handed out cold, hard cash

467
00:36:06,081 --> 00:36:08,820
to anyone who would agree to vote against Anaconda.

468
00:36:10,420 --> 00:36:15,080
When the votes were tallied, Clark's side had won by 1,800 votes.

469
00:36:16,160 --> 00:36:20,420
When Clark's train came into Helena that night, victory night,

470
00:36:20,960 --> 00:36:23,060
he was brought into town in a carriage.

471
00:36:23,061 --> 00:36:28,120
The people unhooked the horses and pulled the carriage into the downtown area,

472
00:36:28,540 --> 00:36:30,040
last chance gulch by hand.

473
00:36:30,580 --> 00:36:34,840
The main bars in Helena were opened on his tab, supposedly 30,000 bucks.

474
00:36:34,940 --> 00:36:38,400
That would buy a lot of alcohol in 1894.

475
00:36:38,700 --> 00:36:42,840
So this unlovable man, for this one time in his life, he was loved.

476
00:36:44,320 --> 00:36:46,180
Emboldened by his victory with Helena,

477
00:36:46,660 --> 00:36:50,500
Clark decided to make another bid for the U.S. Senate in 1898.

478
00:36:50,501 --> 00:36:53,500
Clark knew better than to count on winning the votes.

479
00:36:53,501 --> 00:36:57,500
This time he'd play it safe and buy the votes.

480
00:36:57,501 --> 00:37:07,500
There were rumors that bags of cash were being tossed around like crazy over transoms and in the back rooms.

481
00:37:07,501 --> 00:37:12,500
And there were a lot of people who obviously made off real well.

482
00:37:12,501 --> 00:37:16,500
They tell some very peculiar stories about where that wealth came from.

483
00:37:16,501 --> 00:37:23,500
Some guy suddenly can buy a ranch or a sawmill, and you find out in a few cases that either Clark or some Clark bank or partner

484
00:37:23,501 --> 00:37:26,500
has suddenly become his business partner.

485
00:37:26,501 --> 00:37:28,711
Soon, Daley got his checkbook out.

486
00:37:28,712 --> 00:37:34,500
Daley wasn't running for office himself, but he was determined to stop Clark at any cost.

487
00:37:34,501 --> 00:37:42,500
In the days before soft money and pollsters, Daley and Clark were locked in an escalating battle to outspend one another.

488
00:37:42,501 --> 00:37:43,274
another.

489
00:37:43,275 --> 00:37:51,600
In the 1899-1900 Senate race, which I usually talk about being the fourth major battle

490
00:37:51,601 --> 00:37:57,140
between William Andrews Clark and Marcus Daly, I do believe that William Andrews Clark admitted

491
00:37:57,141 --> 00:38:02,765
to spending over $430,000 on buying votes in the Montana State Legislature.

492
00:38:02,766 --> 00:38:04,040
I think that they

493
00:38:04,041 --> 00:38:08,480
It probably went one, two million easy in bribery.

494
00:38:08,900 --> 00:38:11,300
When we return, the votes are in.

495
00:38:11,301 --> 00:38:16,220
The 25-year feud between William Clark and Marcus Daly ends,

496
00:38:16,580 --> 00:38:19,340
but not without one more big surprise.

497
00:38:20,680 --> 00:38:24,460
The U.S. nickel is actually 75% copper.

498
00:38:24,900 --> 00:38:30,300
The dime, quarter, and half dollar coins contain 91.67% copper,

499
00:38:30,301 --> 00:38:35,460
and the Susan B. Anthony dollar is comprised of 87.5% copper.

500
00:38:36,080 --> 00:38:37,820
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

501
00:38:39,220 --> 00:38:42,760
We now return to Copper Kings on Modern Marvels.

502
00:38:45,860 --> 00:38:47,960
In the winter of 1899,

503
00:38:48,340 --> 00:38:52,560
William Clark and Marcus Daly were locked in a ferocious battle to buy votes.

504
00:38:53,300 --> 00:38:57,060
At stake was a U.S. Senate seat that Clark had been coveting for a decade.

505
00:38:57,061 --> 00:39:05,680
It was common in those days for millionaires to want to establish themselves in almost Roman fashion as a United States senator.

506
00:39:06,180 --> 00:39:07,347
And here was W.A.

507
00:39:07,348 --> 00:39:13,320
Clark, the richest man in his state, who thought that readily translated into politics.

508
00:39:13,940 --> 00:39:20,200
He felt that it was his natural right, and I think society tended to agree that that probably was his natural right.

509
00:39:21,060 --> 00:39:25,100
On January 28th, 1899, the votes were counted.

510
00:39:26,000 --> 00:39:31,340
William A. Clark, one-time miner, price-gouging merchant, and cutthroat financier,

511
00:39:31,700 --> 00:39:34,500
was now Senator William A. Clark.

512
00:39:36,220 --> 00:39:40,660
Clark's hard work and open pocketbook had not only bought a U.S. Senate seat,

513
00:39:40,960 --> 00:39:44,860
it also bought a satisfying measure of revenge on Marcus Daly.

514
00:39:45,400 --> 00:39:48,100
His triumph over his rival looked complete.

515
00:39:49,920 --> 00:39:53,300
But while Clark was openly working on buying votes,

516
00:39:53,301 --> 00:39:56,600
Daly had been secretly working on selling the Anaconda.

517
00:39:57,980 --> 00:40:02,260
In April of 1899, the story broke like a bombshell.

518
00:40:02,900 --> 00:40:08,280
Daly was selling the entire Anaconda operation for the staggering sum of $39 million.

519
00:40:09,140 --> 00:40:12,760
The buyers had ties to a family-run business back east.

520
00:40:13,460 --> 00:40:17,860
The business was Standard Oil, and the family was named Rockefeller.

521
00:40:17,861 --> 00:40:29,280
When you think about Standard Oil actually buying Anaconda, you're looking at something like AOL buying Time Warner.

522
00:40:29,420 --> 00:40:33,300
This is a major event, and the money is very comparable.

523
00:40:34,000 --> 00:40:42,380
Tens of millions of dollars in 1899, 98-99, are worth billions today.

524
00:40:43,280 --> 00:40:46,480
In Butte, the news of the deal was hardly cause for rejoicing.

525
00:40:47,380 --> 00:40:49,740
The locals were shocked and scared.

526
00:40:50,920 --> 00:41:01,260
The barons of Standard Oil, of course, had already made a very, very ugly reputation for themselves in the oil business in Pennsylvania, Ohio, and so on.

527
00:41:01,740 --> 00:41:07,340
So these men, like Henry Rogers and William Rockefeller, were reviled and were deeply distrusted.

528
00:41:07,860 --> 00:41:13,840
Having sold out to ruthless eastern robber barons, Daly was now wealthy beyond reckoning.

529
00:41:13,841 --> 00:41:16,820
but he wouldn't have long to enjoy his wealth.

530
00:41:17,760 --> 00:41:20,900
By the fall of 1900, he was dying.

531
00:41:22,200 --> 00:41:23,300
He had diabetes

532
00:41:23,301 --> 00:41:27,220
and he had gone to Germany for the cure.

533
00:41:27,880 --> 00:41:30,340
At that time, people believed that the waters,

534
00:41:30,720 --> 00:41:32,140
the spring waters in Germany,

535
00:41:32,780 --> 00:41:35,000
different places had curative power.

536
00:41:36,100 --> 00:41:37,660
He came back to New York

537
00:41:37,661 --> 00:41:40,600
and was preparing to come home to Montana

538
00:41:40,601 --> 00:41:42,680
when he became real sick.

539
00:41:42,681 --> 00:41:45,460
Daly never made it back to Montana.

540
00:41:46,180 --> 00:41:48,700
He died in New York in November of 1900.

541
00:41:50,120 --> 00:41:52,500
It was the end of an era in copper mining,

542
00:41:53,120 --> 00:41:56,280
an era when an uneducated immigrant could build an empire

543
00:41:56,281 --> 00:41:58,920
by natural genius and hard work.

544
00:42:00,020 --> 00:42:02,020
But Daly left an enduring mark.

545
00:42:02,740 --> 00:42:06,140
Anaconda would dominate the world copper market for decades.

546
00:42:06,141 --> 00:42:13,640
As for William Clark, his Senate career was short-lived.

547
00:42:14,400 --> 00:42:20,680
After just one term, Montana voters, disgusted by his shameless vote-buying, sent Clark packing.

548
00:42:21,640 --> 00:42:23,760
Clark outlived Daley by 28 years.

549
00:42:24,260 --> 00:42:28,720
His mining and banking empire kept him one of America's richest men throughout his life.

550
00:42:28,721 --> 00:42:38,060
In the decades after Clark N. Daly, the city of Butte fared badly.

551
00:42:38,820 --> 00:42:42,880
Labor strife, low copper prices, and soaring production costs

552
00:42:42,881 --> 00:42:46,280
put Butte into a slump that lasted for much of the 20th century.

553
00:42:48,960 --> 00:42:52,740
By the early 1980s, mining in Butte was history.

554
00:42:53,640 --> 00:42:55,220
Copper prices were in the cellar.

555
00:42:55,221 --> 00:43:04,320
And the famous Anaconda mine had become the infamous Berkeley Pit, a festering open sore steadily filling with toxic wastewater.

556
00:43:05,580 --> 00:43:10,360
Butte and Anaconda were identified as the nation's worst environmental mess.

557
00:43:11,720 --> 00:43:20,980
I suspect by the time cleanup has been completed along the Clark Fork, we will spend upwards of perhaps a billion dollars.

558
00:43:20,981 --> 00:43:23,480
even 100 or 200 years from now,

559
00:43:23,600 --> 00:43:26,940
we'll still be dealing with some of the legacies of this cleanup,

560
00:43:27,480 --> 00:43:30,520
and some of the legacies may be here forever, such as the Berkeley Pit.

561
00:43:30,800 --> 00:43:33,140
The landscape has changed forever,

562
00:43:33,660 --> 00:43:37,000
and you have to understand the balance of mining.

563
00:43:38,200 --> 00:43:40,740
Mining gives America great things,

564
00:43:41,160 --> 00:43:46,360
automobiles, mountain bikes, pretty jewelry, those sorts of things,

565
00:43:46,620 --> 00:43:49,060
but it leaves a terrible scar,

566
00:43:49,061 --> 00:43:52,320
And we have, as a community, had to deal with that.

567
00:43:53,660 --> 00:43:58,080
But if Marcus Daly and William Clark left behind an environmental disaster,

568
00:43:58,560 --> 00:44:00,100
they left other things as well.

569
00:44:01,000 --> 00:44:04,360
An inspiring blueprint for any would-be tycoon.

570
00:44:05,720 --> 00:44:09,080
A classic tale of hard work, the American dream.

571
00:44:10,520 --> 00:44:13,520
Ruthless ambition and rags-to-riches success.

572
00:44:14,640 --> 00:44:18,900
A clash of colossal fortunes and titanic egos.

573
00:44:19,060 --> 00:44:23,060
That too is the legacy of the Copper Kings.

